from science_completion import q,p,m
QUESTIONS=[
q(1,'What is a population?','All organisms of one species in an area.','4.7.1.1'),
q(1,'What is biodiversity?','The variety of species in an ecosystem or on Earth.','4.7.3.1'),
q(1,'What is an abiotic factor?','A non-living environmental factor.','4.7.1.2'),
q(1,'What is a producer?','An organism that synthesises organic molecules, usually by photosynthesis.','4.7.2.1'),
q(1,'What does a food-chain arrow represent?','Transfer of material and energy from the organism eaten to its consumer.','4.7.2.1'),
q(1,'What is an extremophile?','An organism adapted to an extreme environment.','4.7.1.4'),
q(1,'Name the gas collected from anaerobic biogas generators.','Methane.','4.7.2.3'),
q(1,'What is food security?','Having enough food to feed a population.','4.7.5.1'),
q(1,'Which fungus is grown to produce mycoprotein?','Fusarium.','4.7.5.4'),
q(1,'State the trophic level of a primary consumer.','Level 2.','4.7.4.1'),
q(2,'Give two resources for which plants compete.','Light and water.','4.7.1.1'),
q(2,'Give two biotic factors that could reduce a rabbit population.','- A new **predator** can increase deaths.\n- A new **pathogen** can cause disease and reduce survival.','4.7.1.3','2022 2H Q02.1: factor classification'),
q(2,'Give two abiotic factors affecting plant distribution.','Light intensity and soil moisture.','4.7.1.2','2022 2H Q02.1'),
q(2,'Explain why removing pollinating insects can affect a whole community.','- Fewer flowers are pollinated, reducing seed/fruit production.\n- Animals relying on those fruits or seeds may lose food: species are **interdependent**.','4.7.1.1'),
q(2,'Explain how small ears can help an animal in a cold environment.','- They reduce exposed **surface area**.\n- Less energy is transferred to the surroundings, conserving body heat.','4.7.1.4'),
q(2,'Distinguish a structural adaptation from a behavioural adaptation.','- Structural: a physical feature, such as thick fur.\n- Behavioural: an action pattern, such as nocturnal activity avoiding daytime heat.','4.7.1.4'),
q(2,'Why use random coordinates when placing quadrats?','- They reduce **selection bias** towards convenient or obvious locations.\n- Samples are more likely to represent the habitat if sufficiently numerous and spread out.','4.7.2.1','2022 2H Q02.2'),
q(2,'When is a transect more useful than random quadrats alone?','- When investigating how abundance changes **across an environmental gradient**.\n- Regularly spaced quadrats along the line link distribution to distance or measured conditions.','4.7.2.1'),
q(2,'Why do fish numbers fall if dissolved oxygen decreases greatly?','- Fish need oxygen for **aerobic respiration**.\n- Less oxygen limits energy release for essential functions, reducing survival.','4.7.1.2'),
q(2,'Why does a predator peak often occur after the prey peak?','- More prey provides more food, improving predator survival and reproduction.\n- The predator population takes time to increase, so its rise **lags behind** the prey increase.','4.7.2.1'),
q(2,'Explain why dry compost decays slowly.','- Microorganisms need **water** to grow and function.\n- Lack of water reduces enzyme-controlled decomposition.','4.7.2.3'),
q(2,'Why does turning a compost heap speed aerobic decay?','- It supplies **oxygen**.\n- This supports aerobic microbial respiration and activity, increasing decomposition under suitable conditions.','4.7.2.3'),
q(2,'How does photosynthesis contribute to the carbon cycle?','- Plants take up **carbon dioxide** from air.\n- They incorporate its carbon into organic molecules such as glucose.','4.7.2.2'),
q(2,'Explain why peat-free compost can reduce environmental damage.','- Less peat extraction protects specialist **habitats and biodiversity**.\n- It preserves stored carbon that could otherwise be released as carbon dioxide through decay or burning.','4.7.3.3'),
q(2,'State two ways to conserve fish stocks.','- **Catch quotas** limit numbers taken.\n- Appropriate **mesh size** lets small fish escape and reach breeding age.','4.7.5.3'),
q(2,'How can controlling the temperature around livestock improve growth efficiency?','- Less energy is needed to maintain body temperature.\n- More energy/material from food is available for **growth** rather than respiration supporting temperature regulation.','4.7.5.2'),
q(2,'Give two threats to food security other than population growth.','- New **pests or pathogens** reduce crop yields.\n- Failed rainfall or conflict can reduce food production or access.','4.7.5.1'),
q(3,'Five 0.5 m² quadrats contain 4, 6, 3, 7 and 5 plants. Estimate population in a 300 m² habitat.','- Mean = (4+6+3+7+5)/5 = **5 plants per quadrat**.\n- Density = 5/0.5 = **10 plants m⁻²**.\n- Estimate = 10 ×300 = **3000 plants**, assuming samples are representative.','4.7.2.1;MS2b'),
q(3,'Calculate the median, mode and range for counts 2, 3, 3, 7, 10.','- Median: middle value = **3**.\n- Mode: most frequent value = **3**.\n- Range = 10 −2 = **8**.','4.7.2.1;MS2b'),
q(3,'Milk reaches the same pH endpoint in 80 s and 40 s. Compare relative decay rates.','- Rates = 1/80 = **0.0125 s⁻¹**, and 1/40 = **0.025 s⁻¹**.\n- The second rate is **twice** the first.\n- The shorter time represents faster change for the same endpoint.','4.7.2.3;MS1c'),
q(3,'A primary-consumer population gains 60 kg from 750 kg of producer biomass. Calculate efficiency and give one cause of loss.','- Efficiency = 60/750 ×100 = **8%**.\n- Much producer material may not be eaten or absorbed.\n- Absorbed molecules may also be used in respiration, releasing carbon dioxide/water rather than becoming new biomass.','4.7.4.3'),
q(3,'Explain why very high temperature can slow milk-fat breakdown despite faster particle movement.','- Enzyme structure changes at excessive temperatures.\n- The **active site is denatured** and substrate binds less effectively.\n- Fewer successful enzyme reactions occur, so breakdown slows or stops.','4.7.2.3'),
q(3,'Explain how decomposers recycle material.','- They secrete **enzymes** that digest dead matter into soluble molecules outside cells.\n- Products are absorbed and used; microbial **respiration releases carbon dioxide**.\n- Mineral ions return to soil for uptake by plants.','4.7.2.2;4.7.4.1','2023 2H Q04.1'),
q(3,'Describe how water returns from the sea to land and then back to sea.','- Water **evaporates**, then condenses to form clouds.\n- **Precipitation** supplies water to land.\n- Runoff and groundwater flow return water through rivers to sea.','4.7.2.2'),
q(3,'Explain how deforestation can increase atmospheric carbon dioxide.','- Burning wood releases carbon dioxide.\n- Decay of remaining biomass also releases it through respiration.\n- Fewer trees remove carbon dioxide by **photosynthesis**.','4.7.3.4'),
q(3,'Explain why higher biodiversity can improve ecosystem stability.','- Species have more alternative sources of food or shelter.\n- Losing one species is less likely to remove every supporting resource.\n- Interdependent populations may therefore be less vulnerable to a single change.','4.7.3.1'),
q(3,'Describe conditions used to produce mycoprotein.','- Grow **Fusarium** on glucose syrup with suitable nutrients and temperature.\n- Supply **oxygen** for aerobic conditions.\n- Harvest and purify the fungal biomass as protein-rich food.','4.7.5.4'),
q(4,'Describe a valid investigation of plant abundance from a shady hedge into open grassland.','- Lay a transect across the gradient and place equal-sized quadrats at regular distances.\n- Count the species consistently and measure light intensity at each site.\n- Repeat along several comparable transects; record soil moisture or other possible influences.\n- Plot abundance against light/distance and compare patterns without assuming correlation alone proves causation.','4.7.2.1;RPA9'),
q(4,'Describe how to compare milk-fat breakdown at different temperatures.','- Equilibrate equal milk, indicator and alkaline starting mixtures in water baths.\n- Add equal volume/concentration of lipase and time a defined pH endpoint.\n- Keep starting pH, volumes and mixing consistent, repeat each temperature and use a no-enzyme control.\n- Compare mean endpoint times or 1/time rates; use eye protection and safe handling of hot water.','4.7.2.3;RPA10'),
q(4,'Explain why biomass normally decreases up a food chain.','- Some organisms or parts are never eaten.\n- Some ingested material is **egested** without absorption.\n- Respiration uses organic molecules and releases carbon dioxide and water.\n- Other absorbed material is excreted in urine, leaving less to build new biomass at the next level.','4.7.4.3'),
q(4,'Explain how fertiliser pollution can harm aquatic animals.','- Extra mineral ions support rapid algal growth.\n- Shading and later algal death provide more material for decomposition.\n- Microorganisms use dissolved **oxygen in respiration**.\n- Aquatic animals receive insufficient oxygen and can die, reducing biodiversity.','4.7.3.2'),
q(4,'Explain how a warmer, drier climate could change distribution of a moisture-dependent plant.','- Some existing sites become too dry or hot for survival/reproduction.\n- The plant may decline there, while wetter or cooler sites become more suitable.\n- Competitors, pollinators and seed dispersal also affect whether new sites are occupied.\n- Compare actual time-series and habitat data; suitable climate alone does not guarantee successful colonisation.','4.7.2.4'),
q(6,'Plan a valid estimate of a plant population in a meadow and evaluate its reliability.','- Measure habitat area and choose a quadrat of known area.\n- Generate random coordinates across the meadow and apply a consistent counting rule.\n- Use many quadrats, count the target species and calculate mean abundance.\n- Estimate population as mean × habitat area/quadrat area.\n- Repeat or increase samples and consider variability, habitat patchiness and identification errors.\n- State that representativeness limits the estimate; avoid claiming random sampling guarantees an exact total.','4.7.2.1;RPA9','2022 2H Q02.2: original plant-estimation context'),
q(6,'Evaluate planting hedgerows and field margins around a monoculture farm.','- They provide food, shelter and breeding sites for additional species.\n- Habitat connections can support movement, pollinators and natural predators of crop pests.\n- This may increase biodiversity and stability.\n- They occupy land that could grow crops, and maintenance costs time and money.\n- Monitor species richness, crop yield and pest levels before and after, using comparable farms or areas.\n- A reasoned judgement balances measured ecological benefits with food production and economic feasibility.','4.7.3.6'),
q(6,'Evaluate intensive farming that keeps animals warm, restricts movement and provides high-protein feed.','- Warm suitable surroundings reduce energy expenditure on maintaining temperature.\n- Less movement reduces respiration linked to activity, and protein supports growth.\n- More food can become useful biomass, improving output per animal.\n- Crowding or restriction can impair welfare and increase disease transmission; heating/feed have costs and impacts.\n- Compare measured growth, feed use, health and living conditions, not production alone.\n- A justified decision depends on whether efficiency gains are achieved with acceptable welfare and environmental effects.','4.7.5.2'),
q(6,'Evaluate quotas and larger mesh sizes for restoring a declining fish stock.','- Quotas reduce the overall catch and can leave more breeding adults.\n- Larger suitable meshes allow smaller fish to escape and reach maturity.\n- Together they can improve future recruitment, provided suitable habitat and food remain.\n- Fishers may lose short-term income; inappropriate mesh or poorly chosen quotas may fail.\n- Stock monitoring, compliance and enforcement are essential.\n- Use evidence of population recovery and sustainable catches to judge effectiveness rather than assuming any fixed quota works forever.','4.7.5.3')]

